WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:03.690
- I want to help you, AMD, free of charge.

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.850
Just drop by the lab and I'm gonna have

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:08.820
the team show you how to benchmark a GPU

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.940
so then you can choose a price
that reflects its performance

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:16.170
because, frankly, you guys
are all over the road here.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:17.850
The RX 7600,

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:21.060
the card you're launching
today manages to get handily

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.943
outclassed by your own products.

00:00:22.943 --> 00:00:23.776
(record scratching)

00:00:23.776 --> 00:00:26.040
Is what I was going to say.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:31.040
Then, on Monday afternoon,
just 39 hours from launch,

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.570
AMD dropped the price!

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.040
Let's go, gamers!

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:36.270
We're gonna have a rough cut

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:39.210
of our original review
on floatplane.com/ltt

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:40.500
if you're curious, by the way,

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.000
but the gist of it is this:

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.360
we liked the card, but
didn't like the price.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:46.200
Well, now,

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.960
it is $60 less than the launch price

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.850
of its predecessor and it's
within $10 of our suggestion

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:57.450
that we thought would make it
competitive, which is nice,

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.820
but then they also dropped the price

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.740
on some of their last gen products,

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.950
making those even more attractiver.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:06.810
There is a lot to unpack here,

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:10.320
but one thing we know for
sure is that thanks to AMD,

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.200
there are finally some, not one,

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.170
some decent mainstream cards

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:18.827
that gamers can actually afford.

00:01:18.827 --> 00:01:22.320
Let's start with the new kid on the block.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.640
The all-new Navi 33 GPU at
the heart of the 7600 is built

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.040
on AMD's latest RDNA 3 architecture,

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.260
which comes with some key benefits,

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.110
like the addition of AI cores that, well,

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:37.560
don't seem to do much right
now, but in the future,

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.000
we're expecting that to change.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.850
You know, fine wine driver
development and all of that.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:43.320
And right out of the gate,

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.840
RDNA 3 also comes with substantially

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.090
improved ray-tracing performance,

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:50.190
but then so does the competition.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:53.520
And AMD has only made things
more difficult for themselves.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.260
Just last month ,

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:56.850
they were over there throwing rocks

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.010
at NVIDIA over the lack of memory

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:00.930
on their more affordable cards.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:03.517
I guess they forgot about
their own glass house here.

00:02:03.517 --> 00:02:06.060
"But this card is for 1080P

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.790
where eight gigs is enough," says AMD.

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:12.060
Unfortunately, that's not entirely true.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:13.380
Have a look.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:18.380
In "F1 22," AMD pulls off both
a big win and an own goal.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:23.730
Their last gen 6700 XT
leads the entire field,

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:27.270
beating even the 4070 that
costs almost twice as much,

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:30.570
but then their shiny
new 7600 falls squarely

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:35.570
in the middle of the pack
and behind their own 6650 XT,

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.490
a last gen card that at
press time costs anywhere

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:41.910
from 10 to $30 less.

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:44.520
Rough start and definitive proof

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.920
that you should completely
ignore performance rumors

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:48.660
for upcoming products.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:49.650
In "Far Cry 6,"

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:51.600
AMD manages to avoid beating themselves

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:52.890
at their own game by keeping ahead

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:56.370
of their own lower priced
cards, albeit barely.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:57.900
And the same goes for "Hitman 3"

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:01.470
where the most interesting
result is actually the Arc A770.

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:03.330
Intel's software team has clearly

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:04.950
been working their butts
off to the point where

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:08.130
they now kind of sort of have
a card that manages to keep

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.890
up with the competition, or it did anyway.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.440
But can you keep up with this
message from our sponsor?

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:14.280
- The Ridge!

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:15.720
Father's Day is coming up.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:16.553
Luckily,

00:03:16.553 --> 00:03:18.840
Ridge is having one of their
biggest sales of the year.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.520
Give the dads in your life a unique

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:22.680
and functional Ridge wallet or key case

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:23.880
so they can do dad things,

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.830
like brag about their wallet and keys.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.680
Check The Ridge out at the
link below and make dad proud.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.480
- As we tested more and more games,

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:32.580
we realized that the main takeaway

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.530
is that for 1080P gaming,

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:37.770
you really don't need to spend
more than a few hundred bucks

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:40.680
on a replacement for
your aging mid-tier GPU.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.300
Even in "Returnal" and "Cyberpunk,"

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:43.770
we saw average frame rates

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:48.570
above 60 fps across our
entire stable of modern cards,

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:51.360
even if the 1% lows are a bit lower.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.450
With that said, there
is a big asterisk here.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:57.210
Look at the results for
"The Last of Us Part 1."

00:03:57.210 --> 00:04:00.690
The 3060 has been quietly
getting its butt handed

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:02.730
to it the whole time, and then, boom!

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:05.430
It's out in front of the 7600.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:06.450
Interesting.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:08.160
We're gonna talk about that in a moment.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:10.530
First, if you're a 1080P gamer,

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:13.740
AMD's new card seems to slot
into their lineup very nicely,

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:17.250
falling behind its bigger,
older brother, the 6700 XT,

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:20.430
by about 20% in both
price and performance.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:23.490
While it's little, though
still older brother,

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:27.090
the 6650 XT gives up just 5% performance

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:31.020
on average and doesn't net you
much in the way of savings.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:33.540
Given that this is a
newer GPU that will likely

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:35.310
receive driver support for longer,

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:39.270
the 7600 is getting very close
to a recommendation here,

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.490
kinda like how we'd recommend
an insulated water bottle

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:43.110
from lttstore.com.

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:46.500
But, first, we need to talk about VRAM.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:49.500
Both Team Green and Team Red are adamant

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:53.790
that eight gigs of GDDR
6 is enough for 1080P.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:56.160
And yet we found real world examples

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.050
of games that did struggle,

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:00.900
especially those pesky PlayStation ports.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:04.260
This was very apparent
when we saw the 3060 12 gig

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:09.000
suddenly outperforming the
supposedly faster RX 7600.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:10.470
And whether you like it or not,

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:12.390
this is gonna be a trend, gamers.

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:13.740
For over a decade now,

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:15.870
consoles have been what dictates the rate

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:17.820
of growth for hardware requirements.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:19.527
It's no coincidence that the PS5

00:05:19.527 --> 00:05:22.920
and the Xbox Series X both
feature a shared memory

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:26.250
architecture of 16 gigs
and 10 gigs respectively,

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:27.630
and now all of a sudden,

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.730
we're seeing games
developed for these consoles

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:32.550
that can demand significantly more VRAM

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:34.890
than an eight gig GPU can provide.

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:37.620
And with the potential for
a mid-generation refresh

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:38.850
to these consoles,

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:41.400
it's not entirely unrealistic to expect

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.470
that VRAM requirements
could increase further

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:44.640
in the coming years.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:47.280
Take it with a grain of salt,
rumors, and all of that.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:48.900
The issue on top of that, though,

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:51.270
is all of this is just for 1080P.

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:53.580
If you own a 1440P display,

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:57.300
you might desire that extra
VRAM today, right now.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.670
Across our suite of games in 1440P,

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:03.180
our rankings remained largely
similar to the 1080P results,

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:05.220
but it should be noted that the cards

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:08.970
with the extra VRAM tend to
hold their positions better.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.490
Intel in particular starts to pull ahead

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:15.000
and even managed some small
victories where it lost before.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.610
And the 3060 12 gig holds its position

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:19.980
despite its much weaker GPU.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:20.813
Of course,

00:06:20.813 --> 00:06:22.770
we were a lot more upset about all of this

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:25.470
before the 7600 got a new price point

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:29.520
and NVIDIA is clearly
still the worst offender.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:30.353
Moving on.

00:06:30.353 --> 00:06:33.090
We know these are obviously not 4K cards,

00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:34.500
at least not without the help

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:36.780
of their respective
super sampling solutions,

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:38.280
but our pattern continues

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:41.130
with the 3060 12 gig
closing its performance gap

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:44.850
versus the 7600 while
Intel extends its own.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:45.683
To be clear,

00:06:45.683 --> 00:06:47.070
we wouldn't recommend any of these cards

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:50.130
for 4K ultra gaming, say, for the 4070,

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:52.320
but what's cool is that
we no longer have to

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:54.300
say the same thing about ray tracing.

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:57.300
7600 isn't exactly a ray tracing monster,

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:00.690
but it did better than we
expected at 1080P native.

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:01.800
And looking at our results

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:03.870
with FidelityFX Super Resolution,

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:05.820
which has massively improved,

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:09.030
even if it does still
lag behind NVIDIA's DLSS,

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:13.170
you can get a decent
1440P gaming experience.

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:15.000
Although that is still a little

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:18.120
like saying my Civic
can do 130 miles an hour

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:19.830
as long as it's on a steep hill.

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:21.210
So then, performance,

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:22.680
pretty solid for the price

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:26.070
and going with next gen comes
with other upsides too, right?

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:28.020
Like a big leap in efficiency?

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:30.060
No, actually.

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:32.340
20% more performance over last gen

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:36.300
is gonna cost you a 20%
increase in total board power.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.910
I mean, at least it
doesn't overheat, though?

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:40.740
Yes, it doesn't.

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:42.540
And while it isn't the quietest card,

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:45.630
it's not offensively loud
despite its small size.

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:49.440
We also do get display
port 2.1, so that's cool.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:52.800
But AMD removed the one cable USB-C output

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:56.160
that is so awesome on the 7900 series.

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:00.180
So, oh, but what about productivity?

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:01.013
Well,

00:08:01.013 --> 00:08:02.580
NVIDIA dominates Blender thanks

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:04.680
to its excellent CUDA integration.

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:07.710
And while the AV1 encoding
times are competitive

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:10.050
with the more expensive 46 DTI,

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:12.600
that's a nice plus for YouTube streamers.

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:16.710
The RX 7600's H264 and H265 encoding

00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:18.690
are nothing to write home about.

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:20.490
Sorry, Twitch streamers.

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:21.600
And in SPECviewperf,

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:23.550
it sits right in the middle of the pack,

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:26.370
though I doubt that dazzling
productivity performance

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:29.970
would've persuaded the 7600's
target audience anyway.

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:31.680
Not that it really needs it.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:35.907
Assuming you can actually find
one at MSRP, the RX 7600 is,

00:08:35.907 --> 00:08:37.560
and I can't believe I get to

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:39.480
say this for the first time in years,

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:42.390
an attractive mainstream gaming card.

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:43.620
However,

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:46.110
I'm sure we weren't the
only ones who were surprised

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:48.420
by the price change and AMD's partners

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:50.400
may have been caught with board designs

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:52.920
that make this new
price point impractical.

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:55.350
So it could be some time
before the market is flush

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:59.730
with sub $300 GPUs, at
least from this gen.

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:02.460
The good news is AMD has
lots of other options

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:04.650
with fresh price cuts as well.

00:09:04.650 --> 00:09:08.670
For as low as $320 for the 6700 XT,

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:11.010
you'll get a roughly 20 to 25%

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:14.040
performance uplift for
an equal bump in price,

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:15.960
albeit with a higher cost of ownership

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:17.640
from its increased power draw.

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:21.450
The 6650 XT, meanwhile, is
looking like a pretty okay,

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:23.280
similarly priced alternative.

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:27.270
And the 6600 is kind of
the quiet star of the show

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:29.670
if you can get it for the
price we're seeing now.

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:32.220
Just 200 dollaridoos?

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:34.350
And then there's NVIDIA
who has transitioned

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:36.450
from making GPUs for gamers

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:39.660
to making GPUs for fans of NVIDIA.

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:40.500
And now,

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:42.120
it's time to see our original outro

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:46.560
for this video where I low-key
absolutely (bleep) nailed it.

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:49.020
It's not a budget productivity workhorse

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:51.480
nor is it a mainstream
level gaming godsend.

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:54.510
It just kind of sits there

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:59.250
with its main differentiators
being shiny and new.

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:00.480
Is that the play, then?

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:02.550
Try and get people who want the new shiny

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:04.680
and aren't doing much research?

